  <exercise xml:id="ez-8-1-1">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Throughout our work in <xref ref="sec-8-1-linear-poly">Section</xref>, we have focused on approximating the function <m>f(x) = e^x</m>.  In this exercise, we change the function of interest to <m>f(x) = \frac{1}{3}x^3 + \frac{1}{4}x^2 - 2x - 1</m>, and consider the linear and quadratic approximations to <m>f</m> near <m>a = 0</m>.
      </p>

      <p>
        <ol  marker="a.">
          <li>
            <p>
              Determine <m>f'(x)</m> and <m>f''(x)</m> and enter their formulas below.
          

                    <md>
                      <mrow>f(x) \amp= \frac{1}{3}x^3 + \frac{1}{4}x^2 - 2x - 1</mrow>
                      <mrow>f'(x) \amp= <fillin characters="10" /> </mrow>
                      <mrow>f''(x) \amp= <fillin characters="10" /> </mrow>
                    </md>
                  </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              Next, compute <m>f(0)</m>, <m>f'(0)</m>, and <m>f''(0)</m> and enter those values below.

                    <md>
                      <mrow>f(0) \amp= <fillin characters="5" /> </mrow>
                      <mrow>f'(0) \amp= <fillin characters="5" /> </mrow>
                      <mrow>f''(0) \amp= <fillin characters="5" /> </mrow>
                    </md>
            </p>
            
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              Use your work so far to determine the formula for <m>T_1(x)</m>, the tangent line approximation to <m>f(x)</m> at <m>a =0</m> (which satisfies <m>T_1(0) = f(0)</m> and <m>T_1'(0) = f'(0)</m>).
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              Let <m>T_2(x)</m> be the quadratic approximation to <m>f(x)</m> near <m>a = 0</m> that satisfies <m>T_2(0) = f(0)</m>, <m>T_2'(0) = f'(0)</m>, and <m>T_2''(0) = f''(0)</m>.  You might start by letting <m>T_2(x) = c_0 + c_1 x + c_2 x^2</m>, and creating an updated table like the one shown below.
            </p>
            <sidebyside widths="45% 45%">
              <p>
                    <md>
                      <mrow>f(x) \amp= x^3/3 + x^2/4 - 2x - 1</mrow>  
                      <mrow>f'(x) \amp= <fillin characters="10" /> </mrow>
                      <mrow>f''(x) \amp= <fillin characters="10" /> </mrow>
                      <mrow> \amp </mrow>
                      <mrow>f(0) \amp= <fillin characters="5" /></mrow>
                      <mrow>f'(0) \amp= <fillin characters="5" /> </mrow>
                      <mrow>f''(0) \amp= <fillin characters="5" /> </mrow>                     
                    </md>
                  </p>
                  <p>
                    <md>
                      <mrow>T_2(x) \amp= c_0 + c_1 x + c_2 x^2</mrow>
                      <mrow>T_2'(x) \amp= c_1 + 2c_2 x </mrow>
                      <mrow>T_2''(x) \amp= <fillin characters="10" /> </mrow>
                      <mrow> \amp </mrow>
                      <mrow>T_2(0) \amp= <fillin characters="5" /></mrow>
                      <mrow>T_2'(0) \amp= <fillin characters="5" /> </mrow>
                      <mrow>T_2''(0) \amp= <fillin characters="5" /> </mrow>   
                    </md>
                  </p>
                  </sidebyside>
                  <p>
              Use your work in in the table above to find the formula for <m>T_2(x)</m> that satisfies <m>T_2(0) = f(0)</m>, <m>T_2'(0) = f'(0)</m>, and <m>T_2''(0) = f''(0)</m>.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              Plot <m>f(x)</m>, <m>T_1(x)</m>, and <m>T_2(x)</m> on the same axes, centered at <m>a = 0</m>.  What do you notice?
            </p>
          </li>
        </ol>
      </p>
    </statement>
    
    <answer>
 
      <p>
        <ol  marker="a.">
          <li>
              <tabular top="none" bottom="none">
                <row>
                  <cell halign="right"><m>f(x)=</m></cell>
                  <cell><m>\frac{1}{3}x^3 + \frac{1}{4}x^2 - 2x - 1</m></cell>
                </row>
                <row>
                  <cell halign="right"><m>f'(x)=</m></cell>
                  <cell><m>x^2 + \frac{1}{2}x - 2</m></cell>
                </row>                
                <row>
                  <cell halign="right"><m>f''(x)=</m></cell>
                  <cell><m>2x + \frac{1}{2}</m></cell>
                </row>
                <row>
                  <cell halign="right"><m>f(0)=</m></cell>
                  <cell><m>-1</m></cell>
                </row>
                <row>
                  <cell halign="right"><m>f'(0)=</m></cell>
                  <cell><m>-2</m></cell>
                </row>                
                <row>
                  <cell halign="right"><m>f''(0)=</m></cell>
                  <cell><m>\frac{1}{2}</m></cell>
                </row>
              </tabular>

          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              See the bottom half of the table in the previous item.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              <m>T_1(x) = -1 - 2x</m>.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
              <tabular top="none" bottom="none">
                <row>
                  <cell halign="right"><m>f(x)=</m></cell>
                  <cell><m>\frac{1}{3}x^3 + \frac{1}{4}x^2 - 2x - 1</m></cell>
                  <cell halign="right"><m>T_2(x)=</m></cell>
                  <cell><m>c_0 + c_1 x + c_2 x^2</m></cell>
                </row>
                <row>
                  <cell halign="right"><m>f'(x)=</m></cell>
                  <cell><m>x^2 + \frac{1}{2}x - 2</m></cell>
                  <cell halign="right"><m>T_2'(x)=</m></cell>
                  <cell><m>c_1 + 2 c_2 x</m></cell>
                </row>                
                <row>
                  <cell halign="right"><m>f''(x)=</m></cell>
                  <cell><m>2x + \frac{1}{2}</m></cell>
                  <cell halign="right"><m>T_2''(x)=</m></cell>
                  <cell><m>2 c_2</m></cell>
                </row>

                <row>
                  <cell></cell>
                  <cell></cell>
                  <cell></cell>
                  <cell></cell>
                </row>
                <row>
                  <cell halign="right"><m>f(0)=</m></cell>
                  <cell><m>-1</m></cell>
                  <cell halign="right"><m>T_2(0)=</m></cell>
                  <cell><m>c_0</m></cell>
                </row>
                <row>
                  <cell halign="right"><m>f'(0)=</m></cell>
                  <cell><m>-2</m></cell>
                  <cell halign="right"><m>T_2'(0)=</m></cell>
                  <cell><m>c_1</m></cell>
                </row>                
                <row>
                  <cell halign="right"><m>f''(0)=</m></cell>
                  <cell><m>\frac{1}{2}</m></cell>
                  <cell halign="right"><m>T_2''(0)=</m></cell>
                  <cell><m>2c_2</m></cell>
                </row>
              </tabular>
            <p>
              <m>T_2(x) = \frac{1}{4}x^2 - 2x - 1</m>
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              <m>T_2(x)</m> is a better approximation of <m>f(x)</m> than <m>T_1(x)</m> and is better on a wider interval.
            </p>
          </li>
        </ol>
      </p>

    </answer>
    
    <solution>

      <p>
        <ol  marker="a.">
          <li>
              <tabular top="none" bottom="none">
                <row>
                  <cell halign="right"><m>f(x)=</m></cell>
                  <cell><m>\frac{1}{3}x^3 + \frac{1}{4}x^2 - 2x - 1</m></cell>
                </row>
                <row>
                  <cell halign="right"><m>f'(x)=</m></cell>
                  <cell><m>x^2 + \frac{1}{2}x - 2</m></cell>
                </row>                
                <row>
                  <cell halign="right"><m>f''(x)=</m></cell>
                  <cell><m>2x + \frac{1}{2}</m></cell>
                </row>
                <row>
                  <cell halign="right"><m>f(0)=</m></cell>
                  <cell><m>-1</m></cell>
                </row>
                <row>
                  <cell halign="right"><m>f'(0)=</m></cell>
                  <cell><m>-2</m></cell>
                </row>                
                <row>
                  <cell halign="right"><m>f''(0)=</m></cell>
                  <cell><m>\frac{1}{2}</m></cell>
                </row>
              </tabular>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              See the bottom half of the table in the previous item.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              We know that <m>T_1(x)</m> is the tangent line approximation given by <m>T_1(x) = f(0) + f'(0) (x-0)</m>, so <m>T_1(x) = -1 - 2x</m>.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
              <tabular top="none" bottom="none">
                <row>
                  <cell halign="right"><m>f(x)=</m></cell>
                  <cell><m>\frac{1}{3}x^3 + \frac{1}{4}x^2 - 2x - 1</m></cell>
                  <cell halign="right"><m>T_2(x)=</m></cell>
                  <cell><m>c_0 + c_1 x + c_2 x^2</m></cell>
                </row>
                <row>
                  <cell halign="right"><m>f'(x)=</m></cell>
                  <cell><m>x^2 + \frac{1}{2}x - 2</m></cell>
                  <cell halign="right"><m>T_2'(x)=</m></cell>
                  <cell><m>c_1 + 2 c_2 x</m></cell>
                </row>                
                <row>
                  <cell halign="right"><m>f''(x)=</m></cell>
                  <cell><m>2x + \frac{1}{2}</m></cell>
                  <cell halign="right"><m>T_2''(x)=</m></cell>
                  <cell><m>2 c_2</m></cell>
                </row>

                <row>
                  <cell></cell>
                  <cell></cell>
                  <cell></cell>
                  <cell></cell>
                </row>
                <row>
                  <cell halign="right"><m>f(0)=</m></cell>
                  <cell><m>-1</m></cell>
                  <cell halign="right"><m>T_2(0)=</m></cell>
                  <cell><m>c_0</m></cell>
                </row>
                <row>
                  <cell halign="right"><m>f'(0)=</m></cell>
                  <cell><m>-2</m></cell>
                  <cell halign="right"><m>T_2'(0)=</m></cell>
                  <cell><m>c_1</m></cell>
                </row>                
                <row>
                  <cell halign="right"><m>f''(0)=</m></cell>
                  <cell><m>\frac{1}{2}</m></cell>
                  <cell halign="right"><m>T_2''(0)=</m></cell>
                  <cell><m>2c_2</m></cell>
                </row>
              </tabular>
            <p>
              We find <m>T_2(x)</m> that satisfies <m>T_2(0) = f(0)</m>, <m>T_2'(0) = f'(0)</m>, and <m>T_2''(0) = f''(0)</m> by now observing that <m>c_0 = -1</m>, <m>c_1 = -2</m>, and <m>2c_2 = \frac{1}{2}</m>, so <m>c_2 = \frac{1}{4}</m>.  Thus <m>T_2(x) = \frac{1}{4}x^2 - 2x - 1</m>
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              If we zoom in, we see that near <m>a = 0</m>, <m>T_2(x)</m> is a better approximation of <m>f(x)</m> than <m>T_1(x)</m> and is better on a wider interval.
            </p>
          </li>
        </ol>
      </p>

      
    </solution>
  </exercise>

  <exercise>
    <statement>
      <p>
        In this exercise, we extend our work in <xref ref="ez-8-1-1">Exercise</xref>.  We continue to consider the function <m>f(x) = \frac{1}{3}x^3 + \frac{1}{4}x^2 - 2x - 1</m>, but now build the cubic (degree <m>3</m>) approximation to <m>f</m> near <m>a = 0</m>.
      </p>

      <ol>
        <li>
          <p>Let <m>T_3(x) = k_0 + k_1x + k_2x^2 + k_3x^3</m> and determine <m>T_3'(x)</m>, <m>T_3''(x)</m>, and <m>T_3'''(x)</m>.  In addition, determine <m>f'''(x)</m>.  Record your results below, along with the values of each of these functions at <m>a = 0</m>.</p>

          <sidebyside widths="45% 45%">
            <p>
              <md>
                <mrow>f(x) \amp= x^3/3 + x^2/4 - 2x - 1</mrow>  
                <mrow>f'(x) \amp= <fillin characters="10" /> </mrow>
                <mrow>f''(x) \amp= <fillin characters="10" /> </mrow>
                <mrow>f'''(x) \amp= <fillin characters="10" /> </mrow>
                <mrow> \amp </mrow>
                <mrow>f(0) \amp= <fillin characters="5" /></mrow>
                <mrow>f'(0) \amp= <fillin characters="5" /> </mrow>
                <mrow>f''(0) \amp= <fillin characters="5" /> </mrow>   
                <mrow>f'''(0) \amp= <fillin characters="5" /> </mrow>                   
              </md>
            </p>
            <p>
              <md>
                <mrow>T_3(x) \amp= k_0 + k_1 x + k_2 x^2 + k_3 x^3</mrow>
                <mrow>T_3'(x) \amp= <fillin characters="10" /> </mrow>
                <mrow>T_3''(x) \amp= <fillin characters="10" /> </mrow>
                <mrow>T_3'''(x) \amp= <fillin characters="10" /> </mrow>
                <mrow> \amp </mrow>
                <mrow>T_3(0) \amp= <fillin characters="5" /></mrow>
                <mrow>T_3'(0) \amp= <fillin characters="5" /> </mrow>
                <mrow>T_3''(0) \amp= <fillin characters="5" /> </mrow>
                <mrow>T_3'''(0) \amp= <fillin characters="5" /> </mrow>     
              </md>
            </p>
          </sidebyside>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            Use your work in above to find the formula for <m>T_3(x)</m> that satisfies <m>T_3(0) = f(0)</m>, <m>T_3'(0) = f'(0)</m>, <m>T_3''(0) = f''(0)</m>, and <m>T_3'''(0) = f'''(0)</m>.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
            <p>
              Plot <m>f(x)</m>, <m>T_1(x)</m>, <m>T_2(x)</m>, and <m>T_3(x)</m> on the same axes, centered at <m>a = 0</m>.  What do you notice?
            </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            What do you expect the degree <m>4</m> approximation to <m>f(x)</m>, <m>T_4(x)</m> to be?  Why?
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            What do you expect will happen if we find the degree <m>6</m> approximation to any function <m>f(x)</m> that is itself a degree <m>6</m> polynomial?
          </p>
        </li>
      </ol>
    </statement>
    
    <answer>

      <ol>
        <li>
              <tabular top="none" bottom="none">
                <row>
                  <cell halign="right"><m>f(x)=</m></cell>
                  <cell><m>\frac{1}{3}x^3 + \frac{1}{4}x^2 - 2x - 1</m></cell>
                  <cell halign="right"><m>T_3(x)=</m></cell>
                  <cell><m>k_0 + k_1 x + k_2 x^2 + k_3 x^3</m></cell>
                </row>
                <row>
                  <cell halign="right"><m>f'(x)=</m></cell>
                  <cell><m>x^2 + \frac{1}{2}x - 2</m></cell>
                  <cell halign="right"><m>T_3'(x)=</m></cell>
                  <cell><m>k_1 + 2 k_2 x + 3 k_3 x^2</m></cell>
                </row>                
                <row>
                  <cell halign="right"><m>f''(x)=</m></cell>
                  <cell><m>2x + \frac{1}{2}</m></cell>
                  <cell halign="right"><m>T_3''(x)=</m></cell>
                  <cell><m>2 k_2 + 6k_3 x</m></cell>
                </row>
                <row>
                  <cell halign="right"><m>f'''(x)=</m></cell>
                  <cell><m>2</m></cell>
                  <cell halign="right"><m>T_3'''(x)=</m></cell>
                  <cell><m>6k_3</m></cell>
                </row>
                <row>
                  <cell></cell>
                  <cell></cell>
                  <cell></cell>
                  <cell></cell>
                </row>
                <row>
                  <cell halign="right"><m>f(0)=</m></cell>
                  <cell><m>-1</m></cell>
                  <cell halign="right"><m>T_3(0)=</m></cell>
                  <cell><m>k_0</m></cell>
                </row>
                <row>
                  <cell halign="right"><m>f'(0)=</m></cell>
                  <cell><m>-2</m></cell>
                  <cell halign="right"><m>T_3'(0)=</m></cell>
                  <cell><m>k_1</m></cell>
                </row>                
                <row>
                  <cell halign="right"><m>f''(0)=</m></cell>
                  <cell><m>\frac{1}{2}</m></cell>
                  <cell halign="right"><m>T_3''(0)=</m></cell>
                  <cell><m>2k_2</m></cell>
                </row>
                <row>
                  <cell halign="right"><m>f'''(0)=</m></cell>
                  <cell><m>2</m></cell>
                  <cell halign="right"><m>T_3''(0)=</m></cell>
                  <cell><m>6k_3</m></cell>
                </row>
              </tabular>

        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            <m>T_3(x) = \frac{1}{3}x^3 + \frac{1}{4}x^2 - 2x - 1</m>.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
            <p>
              <m>T_3(x) = f(x)</m> so <m>T_3(x)</m> is identical to the original function.
            </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            <m>T_4(x) = T_3(x) = f(x)</m>.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            That the degree <m>6</m> approximation will be the original polynomial function itself.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ol>
      
    </answer>
    
    <solution>

      <ol>
        <li>
              <tabular top="none" bottom="none">
                <row>
                  <cell halign="right"><m>f(x)=</m></cell>
                  <cell><m>\frac{1}{3}x^3 + \frac{1}{4}x^2 - 2x - 1</m></cell>
                  <cell halign="right"><m>T_3(x)=</m></cell>
                  <cell><m>k_0 + k_1 x + k_2 x^2 + k_3 x^3</m></cell>
                </row>
                <row>
                  <cell halign="right"><m>f'(x)=</m></cell>
                  <cell><m>x^2 + \frac{1}{2}x - 2</m></cell>
                  <cell halign="right"><m>T_3'(x)=</m></cell>
                  <cell><m>k_1 + 2 k_2 x + 3 k_3 x^2</m></cell>
                </row>                
                <row>
                  <cell halign="right"><m>f''(x)=</m></cell>
                  <cell><m>2x + \frac{1}{2}</m></cell>
                  <cell halign="right"><m>T_3''(x)=</m></cell>
                  <cell><m>2 k_2 + 6k_3 x</m></cell>
                </row>
                <row>
                  <cell halign="right"><m>f'''(x)=</m></cell>
                  <cell><m>2</m></cell>
                  <cell halign="right"><m>T_3'''(x)=</m></cell>
                  <cell><m>6k_3</m></cell>
                </row>
                <row>
                  <cell></cell>
                  <cell></cell>
                  <cell></cell>
                  <cell></cell>
                </row>
                <row>
                  <cell halign="right"><m>f(0)=</m></cell>
                  <cell><m>-1</m></cell>
                  <cell halign="right"><m>T_3(0)=</m></cell>
                  <cell><m>k_0</m></cell>
                </row>
                <row>
                  <cell halign="right"><m>f'(0)=</m></cell>
                  <cell><m>-2</m></cell>
                  <cell halign="right"><m>T_3'(0)=</m></cell>
                  <cell><m>k_1</m></cell>
                </row>                
                <row>
                  <cell halign="right"><m>f''(0)=</m></cell>
                  <cell><m>\frac{1}{2}</m></cell>
                  <cell halign="right"><m>T_3''(0)=</m></cell>
                  <cell><m>2k_2</m></cell>
                </row>
                <row>
                  <cell halign="right"><m>f'''(0)=</m></cell>
                  <cell><m>2</m></cell>
                  <cell halign="right"><m>T_3''(0)=</m></cell>
                  <cell><m>6k_3</m></cell>
                </row>
              </tabular>

        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            We see that <m>T_3(0) = f(0)</m> implies <m>k_0 = -1</m>; <m>T_3'(0) = f'(0)</m> implies <m>k_1 = -1</m>; <m>T_3''(0) = f''(0)</m> tells us <m>2k_2 = \frac{1}{2}</m>, so <m>k_2 = \frac{1}{4}</m>; and <m>T_3'''(0) = f'''(0)</m> shows that <m>6k_3 = 2</m>, so <m>k_3 = \frac{1}{3}</m>.  Thus <m>T_3(x) = \frac{1}{3}x^3 + \frac{1}{4}x^2 - 2x - 1</m>.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
            <p>
              We notice that <m>T_3(x) = f(x)</m> so <m>T_3(x)</m> perfectly approximates <m>f(x)</m> for every value of <m>x</m>.
            </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            We expect that <m>T_4(x) = T_3(x) = f(x)</m> because there's no better polynomial approximation to <m>f(x)</m> than <m>T_3(x)</m>.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            If we find the degree <m>6</m> approximation to any function <m>f(x)</m> that is itself a degree <m>6</m> polynomial, we expect that the degree <m>6</m> approximation will be the original polynomial function itself.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ol>
      
    </solution>
  </exercise>

